\title{Practical Guide to this Website}

\p{This website is a \em{forest of [evergreen notes](https://www.forester-notes.org/tfmt-0003/)}, made using the [Forester](https://www.forester-notes.org/index/) tool created and maintained by [Jon Sterling](https://www.jonmsterling.com/index/) and [Kento Okura](https://github.com/kentookura).}

\p{The main difference between this site and a typical way to organise information is that the hierarchy is \em{relative} instead of absolute. For instance, in a set of notes written in #{\LaTeX}, you have the hierarchy}

\figure{\tex{\startverb\usepackage{tikz}\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}\stopverb}{\startverb
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={draw, rounded corners, inner sep=4pt, font=\sffamily},
  level 1/.style={level distance=14mm},
  level 2/.style={level distance=16mm, sibling distance=27mm},
  edge from parent/.style={draw, -{Stealth}}
]
\node {Section}
  child {node {Subsection}
    child {node {Definitions}}
    child {node {Discussions}}
    child {node {Theorems}}
    child {node {Lemmas}}
  };
\end{tikzpicture}
\stopverb}}

\p{Whereas here, all pages you can view (a.k.a. trees) exist in an interconnected network where you can go "up a level" or "down a level" but do not have an absolute place in the hierarchy (if you wish, the poset of trees, ordered by inclusion, is not necessarily graded). For example, a theorem can exist as a subtree of two separate trees.}

\subtree{\title{Practical Guide to Use}

\p{You can navigate around this site through its hyperlinks (similar to a wiki), or by searching with \code{Ctrl + K}.}

\p{By the section headers, you may see a code like “[AB12]”. Clicking that link will take you to that tree/section as its own tree/page without the surrounding content.
}

\p{
At the bottom of a page, you may see any of the following headings containing links to other pages:
}
\ul{
    \li{“\strong{Context}” links up to any page in which the current page is embedded. For example, the context link of a given section will bring up to the lecture it is contained in.}
    \li{“\strong{Related}” contains links to pages referred to in the current page.}
    \li{“\strong{Backlinks}” contains links to pages that refer to the current page.}
    \li{“\strong{References}” links to external resources and books referred to in the page.}
    }
}